Hmm, actually I recall the JS reference solution used a different approach — it checked each direction separately, iterating the grid for each direction. Either way, the result is the same as long as the order is consistent. Let me write it like this: for each word, for each direction, scan rows top-to-bottom, columns left-to-right, and return the first match.

For "rust" bottom-to-top: start [5,9] end [2,9]: row 5 column 9 = r (rixilelhrs column 9 = r), row 4 column 9 = u (pbwasqroua column 9 = u), row 3 column 9 = s (oivokprjsm column 9 = s), row 2 column 9 = t (camdcimgtc column 9 = t). Yes.

For "lisp" bottom-left to top-right: start [6,3] end [3,6]: row 6 column 3 = l (wolcqlirpc column 3 = l), row 5 column 4 = i (rixilelhrs column 4 = i), row 4 column 5 = s (pbwasqroua column 5 = s), row 3 column 6 = p (oivokprjsm column 6 = p). Yes.

For "ruby" top-right to bottom-left: start [6,8] end [9,5]: row 6 column 8 = r (wolcqlirpc column 8 = r), row 7 column 7 = u (screeaumgr column 7 = u), row 8 column 6 = b (alxhpburyi column 6 = b), row 9 column 5 = y (jalaycalmp column 5 = y). Yes.

Now, could "ruby" also be found in another direction earlier in the scan? For example, top-left to bottom-right from some cell? Probably not; the canonical solution passes these tests with the standard order.

Note: the constructor takes grid, not words. find(words) returns an object keyed by word with start/end as [row, column] 1-indexed, or undefined if not found.

Also, check "ecmascript" top-to-bottom start [1,10] end [10,10]: column 10 of each row: e c m a s r i t p t — yes, ecmascript.

